WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.789
This is the executive
briefing for Book 18 of the

3
00:00:03.789 --> 00:00:07.725
Federal Modernization Workbook,
Application Modernization.

4
00:00:07.725 --> 00:00:12.213
It's for the people who make the
calls: the CIO, the chief architect,

5
00:00:12.213 --> 00:00:18.225
portfolio owners, the CISO, the privacy
officer, authorizing officials and sponsors.

6
00:00:18.225 --> 00:00:19.675
The argument is simple.

7
00:00:19.675 --> 00:00:24.300
An agency with thousands of legacy
applications can't rewrite its way out.

8
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.918
It decides, one application at a time,
what each one becomes, and builds the

9
00:00:28.918 --> 00:00:33.612
ones that change on one architecture,
with the data proven and the AI owned.

10
00:00:33.612 --> 00:00:36.849
I'll take four questions:
why, what, who and how.

11
00:00:36.849 --> 00:00:41.225
Each decision slide names the decision,
its owner and when it's needed.

12
00:00:41.225 --> 00:00:43.550
Let's start with where the money goes.

13
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.550
This matters because most of the
IT budget is already spoken for.

14
00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:52.724
G A O puts federal IT spending above
a hundred billion dollars a year,

15
00:00:52.724 --> 00:00:56.600
and about eighty percent of it
keeps existing systems running.

16
00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:59.200
That share has barely moved in six years.

17
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.573
G A O's lists of the most critical
legacy systems get the headlines,

18
00:01:03.573 --> 00:01:06.812
but eleven systems are a
small slice of that cost.

19
00:01:06.812 --> 00:01:11.212
The bulk sits in thousands of ordinary
applications, all of them aging.

20
00:01:11.212 --> 00:01:14.787
And the gap G A O keeps
finding isn't technology.

21
00:01:14.787 --> 00:01:17.576
It's planning, decided
one system at a time.

22
00:01:17.576 --> 00:01:19.730
So why not just replace them all?

23
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.342
Because big rewrites keep failing,
and for structural reasons.

24
00:01:24.342 --> 00:01:26.405
Value only arrives at the end.

25
00:01:26.405 --> 00:01:31.618
The real specification is buried in the
old code and in its users' workarounds.

26
00:01:31.618 --> 00:01:35.643
And all the risk lands on one
cut-over, with no easy way back.

27
00:01:35.643 --> 00:01:38.431
Federal policy already
points the other way.

28
00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:43.066
Circular A one-thirty asks for useful
segments with a narrow scope and a brief

29
00:01:43.066 --> 00:01:47.992
duration, and modular contracting asks for
increments that each work on their own.

30
00:01:47.992 --> 00:01:52.175
So the decision belongs to the
CIO, who approves the contracts:

31
00:01:52.175 --> 00:01:56.029
no proposal that delivers
nothing until a single switch.

32
00:01:56.029 --> 00:01:58.820
If not the whole program, what's the unit?

33
00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:01.983
The unit of decision is the application.

34
00:02:01.983 --> 00:02:05.116
A mission area is too coarse,
and a platform can't be

35
00:02:05.116 --> 00:02:08.632
retired until every application
on it has been decided.

36
00:02:08.632 --> 00:02:13.701
An application has an owner, a data set,
a code base and an authorization boundary,

37
00:02:13.701 --> 00:02:16.695
so it's something you can
actually decide about.

38
00:02:16.695 --> 00:02:20.868
The figure shows the whole
argument: triage every application,

39
00:02:20.868 --> 00:02:23.196
leave many of them alone on purpose,

40
00:02:23.196 --> 00:02:27.770
and build the ones that change on one
target, governed and run in waves.

41
00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:31.760
That makes the first deliverable
an inventory, with an owner and a

42
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.045
proposed disposition for each
application, not a target-state design.

43
00:02:36.045 --> 00:02:38.340
Next, the six choices.

44
00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:44.949
Every application gets one of six
dispositions: retire, retain, rehost,

45
00:02:44.949 --> 00:02:50.303
replatform, refactor or rebuild,
in rising order of cost and risk.

46
00:02:50.303 --> 00:02:53.684
The rule is the cheapest one
that meets the mission need,

47
00:02:53.684 --> 00:02:55.890
and every step up has to be justified.

48
00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.128
Here's the highpoint.

49
00:02:57.128 --> 00:03:01.164
Two of the six, retire and
retain, aren't projects at all.

50
00:03:01.164 --> 00:03:04.364
They need an owner's decision,
not an engineering team,

51
00:03:04.364 --> 00:03:07.853
and in most portfolios they're
where the early savings are.

52
00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:11.752
A portfolio where nothing is retained
probably hasn't been triaged.

53
00:03:11.752 --> 00:03:13.874
So fund decisions as well as projects,

54
00:03:13.874 --> 00:03:17.364
including the decommissioning
that makes a retirement real.

55
00:03:17.364 --> 00:03:19.540
But how do you decide thousands?

56
00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:23.422
You can't study thousands
of applications one by one;

57
00:03:23.422 --> 00:03:25.753
the studies would outlive the systems.

58
00:03:25.753 --> 00:03:31.189
So triage is a scored rubric, filled from
data the agency mostly already holds,

59
00:03:31.189 --> 00:03:36.540
like each system's security categorization,
its support dates and its costs.

60
00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:41.803
A decision table proposes a disposition,
and the owners confirm or contest it.

61
00:03:41.803 --> 00:03:45.259
Only the high-stakes and
disputed cases get a deep study,

62
00:03:45.259 --> 00:03:47.964
which is how a rubric
stays careful at scale.

63
00:03:47.964 --> 00:03:50.373
It's program design, calibrated first on

64
00:03:50.373 --> 00:03:53.365
applications your architects
already know well.

65
00:03:53.365 --> 00:03:58.277
Leadership's job is to approve the rubric
once, then rule on what it sends up.

66
00:03:58.277 --> 00:04:01.270
Now, where the changed applications land.

67
00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:04.387
If every project picks its own stack,

68
00:04:04.387 --> 00:04:07.858
you trade one legacy problem
for hundreds of new ones.

69
00:04:07.858 --> 00:04:13.015
So the applications that change land on
one reference architecture: Java and

70
00:04:13.015 --> 00:04:18.333
Spring Boot services, React front ends,
published A P I contracts, and containers.

71
00:04:18.333 --> 00:04:21.541
These are the program's
choices, not a federal rule,

72
00:04:21.541 --> 00:04:24.358
picked for long support
and a big labor pool.

73
00:04:24.358 --> 00:04:26.456
The catch is that stacks age fast;

74
00:04:26.456 --> 00:04:31.120
some frameworks get only about a year
of open-source support per release.

75
00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.762
So approve one target
and a way to change it,

76
00:04:33.762 --> 00:04:38.895
with the chief architect owning the list
and upgrades budgeted as recurring work.

77
00:04:38.895 --> 00:04:41.130
How do you get there without a rewrite?

78
00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.771
You replace in increments, with
what's called the strangler fig.

79
00:04:45.771 --> 00:04:49.363
A façade sits in front of the
legacy application and routes

80
00:04:49.363 --> 00:04:52.805
each request either to the old
system or to a new service.

81
00:04:52.805 --> 00:04:55.802
Functions move one at a time
behind the same contract,

82
00:04:55.802 --> 00:04:59.480
until nothing depends on the old
system and it can be switched off.

83
00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:03.015
Rollback stays possible until
the old tables are removed,

84
00:05:03.015 --> 00:05:04.930
so that's a deliberate last step.

85
00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:09.055
It doesn't fit everything; the
requests have to be interceptable.

86
00:05:09.055 --> 00:05:13.000
So at design review, ask every
refactor to name its façade,

87
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.717
its first function, its plan for
shared data and when rollback ends.

88
00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:19.430
Then, the data.

89
00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:25.143
Code can move a piece at a time, but
data moves once, and data errors hide.

90
00:05:25.143 --> 00:05:27.715
A date that lost its
time of day may not show

91
00:05:27.715 --> 00:05:30.581
up until someone's record
is wrong months later.

92
00:05:30.581 --> 00:05:34.256
So the move isn't finished
until it's proven, three ways,

93
00:05:34.256 --> 00:05:39.771
chosen so their blind spots don't overlap:
row counts, a checksum over every value,

94
00:05:39.771 --> 00:05:44.617
and totals the business recognizes,
computed separately on each side.

95
00:05:44.617 --> 00:05:47.688
That last one matters,
because a program office can

96
00:05:47.688 --> 00:05:50.831
judge its monthly totals in
a way it can't judge a hash.

97
00:05:50.831 --> 00:05:53.431
The decision: no cut-over until the owner

98
00:05:53.431 --> 00:05:56.268
and the authorizing
official see all three.

99
00:05:56.268 --> 00:05:58.090
And during the transition?

100
00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:01.594
Old and new often run
side by side for months,

101
00:06:01.594 --> 00:06:06.490
and there's a tempting shortcut: have
the application write to both databases.

102
00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:07.178
Don't.

103
00:06:07.178 --> 00:06:12.027
The two writes don't share a transaction,
so under load the copies drift, quietly.

104
00:06:12.027 --> 00:06:15.903
Instead, keep one writer per
table and replicate from it.

105
00:06:15.903 --> 00:06:20.490
One more thing: the target engine decides
which conversion tools you can use,

106
00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:22.528
and for DB2 the options are narrow.

107
00:06:22.528 --> 00:06:24.722
So forbid dual writes as a standard,

108
00:06:24.722 --> 00:06:28.428
and settle the engine before
the wave plan commits to dates.

109
00:06:28.428 --> 00:06:32.040
Now, the newest part of
the architecture: AI.

110
00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:36.694
AI belongs in the architecture as
a component behind one interface,

111
00:06:36.694 --> 00:06:40.040
so providers and models can
change without changing code.

112
00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:44.396
Most uses, like drafting text or
answering from agency content,

113
00:06:44.396 --> 00:06:46.490
change nothing by themselves.

114
00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:48.802
Agents are different, because they act.

115
00:06:48.802 --> 00:06:52.349
You can't count on a model
refusing a hidden instruction,

116
00:06:52.349 --> 00:06:57.361
so the design limits what a steered agent
can do: an identity and a human sponsor,

117
00:06:57.361 --> 00:07:01.602
a short list of allowed tools, a step
limit, and an audit of every call.

118
00:07:01.602 --> 00:07:05.065
And the big one: no agent
writes to a system of record.

119
00:07:05.065 --> 00:07:09.528
It proposes, a person approves,
and the service applies the change.

120
00:07:09.528 --> 00:07:11.420
Next, compliance.

121
00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:15.433
Compliance is usually a
package assembled at the end.

122
00:07:15.433 --> 00:07:17.842
Modernization is the
moment to change that,

123
00:07:17.842 --> 00:07:21.257
because you're choosing the
platform and the pipeline anyway.

124
00:07:21.257 --> 00:07:26.275
A control the platform implements once
is inherited by every application on it,

125
00:07:26.275 --> 00:07:30.532
and a pipeline that keeps its results
writes the evidence as it goes.

126
00:07:30.532 --> 00:07:35.856
Circular A one-thirty also turns legacy
into a compliance question: systems that

127
00:07:35.856 --> 00:07:39.272
can't be protected, including
unsupported software,

128
00:07:39.272 --> 00:07:41.496
get top priority for replacement.

129
00:07:41.496 --> 00:07:45.657
So a retained application on
unsupported software is a finding.

130
00:07:45.657 --> 00:07:49.421
It needs a plan, a risk
acceptance and a review date.

131
00:07:49.421 --> 00:07:51.800
And two officials need to hear early.

132
00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:55.188
Those are the privacy
and records officials.

133
00:07:55.188 --> 00:07:58.927
Modernization changes how
personal information flows,

134
00:07:58.927 --> 00:08:03.779
and the events that reopen their work
are predictable: a new database, a new

135
00:08:03.779 --> 00:08:08.950
data flow, an AI feature, production data
copied into test, and retiring a system.

136
00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:11.050
Retirement is the sneaky one.

137
00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:14.731
Switching off an application is
a decision about its records,

138
00:08:14.731 --> 00:08:18.412
and a migration that drops
history may be destroying records.

139
00:08:18.412 --> 00:08:20.488
These judgments can't be automated.

140
00:08:20.488 --> 00:08:23.788
So name those events, and
route them to the privacy and

141
00:08:23.788 --> 00:08:27.613
records officials at design
review, before anything is built.

142
00:08:27.613 --> 00:08:30.600
That brings us to who
governs the architecture.

143
00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:34.278
A target architecture
holds across hundreds

144
00:08:34.278 --> 00:08:36.975
of teams only if each
decision has an owner.

145
00:08:36.975 --> 00:08:40.182
No federal rule demands an
architecture review board,

146
00:08:40.182 --> 00:08:44.102
but several demand what one
produces: an enterprise architecture,

147
00:08:44.102 --> 00:08:47.737
and controlled change with
security and privacy at the table.

148
00:08:47.737 --> 00:08:50.300
The rule here is one decider per decision.

149
00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:53.575
The chief architect decides
standards and designs.

150
00:08:53.575 --> 00:08:56.500
Application owners decide dispositions.

151
00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:59.234
The authorizing official
alone accepts risk,

152
00:08:59.234 --> 00:09:02.112
and observes the board
rather than sitting on it.

153
00:09:02.112 --> 00:09:06.450
And the CIO, not the board,
approves what the agency buys.

154
00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:09.925
So the CIO charters the
board and names its chair.

155
00:09:09.925 --> 00:09:11.510
Then come the gates.

156
00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:17.111
Every application that changes
passes four gates: its disposition,

157
00:09:17.111 --> 00:09:20.922
a design review, every pull
request, and every release.

158
00:09:20.922 --> 00:09:25.073
Two are meetings and two are
automation, and that's deliberate.

159
00:09:25.073 --> 00:09:29.174
The daily ones run in the pipeline,
on protected branches that bind

160
00:09:29.174 --> 00:09:33.498
administrators too, because a gate
that's run as a meeting gets skipped.

161
00:09:33.498 --> 00:09:37.629
Each gate leaves proof, so the evidence
builds up as the work happens,

162
00:09:37.629 --> 00:09:41.023
instead of screenshots the
month before an assessment.

163
00:09:41.023 --> 00:09:44.610
One threshold belongs to the
authorizing official alone:

164
00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:48.647
when a change is significant enough
to reopen the authorization.

165
00:09:48.647 --> 00:09:51.586
Set it early, and let
design review apply it.

166
00:09:51.586 --> 00:09:53.260
Now, how it runs.

167
00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:57.860
The book ships a small, runnable
reference implementation:

168
00:09:57.860 --> 00:10:00.846
a case service with a
contract test, a front end,

169
00:10:00.846 --> 00:10:05.560
a migration tool with all three checks,
and an agent that can only propose.

170
00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:08.772
Its tests prove the checks by
breaking things on purpose.

171
00:10:08.772 --> 00:10:11.272
Change one amount, and two checks fail.

172
00:10:11.272 --> 00:10:14.734
Truncate one name, and only
the checksum catches it.

173
00:10:14.734 --> 00:10:17.697
Just as important, it says
what it doesn't prove.

174
00:10:17.697 --> 00:10:21.897
It has never run against a
real DB2 or Oracle database,

175
00:10:21.897 --> 00:10:25.523
a real model, or production,
and it has no sign-in.

176
00:10:25.523 --> 00:10:27.884
So copy the pattern, not the claim.

177
00:10:27.884 --> 00:10:31.630
Next, the frameworks your
architects already use.

178
00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:35.770
Many architecture offices
speak TOGAF or Zachman,

179
00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:38.030
so the book maps its work onto both.

180
00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:40.534
The short version: TOGAF
tells you the order,

181
00:10:40.534 --> 00:10:43.942
and Zachman tells you whether
you've described everything.

182
00:10:43.942 --> 00:10:46.030
But neither one is the requirement.

183
00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:50.768
Circular A one-thirty asks for an
enterprise architecture with a baseline,

184
00:10:50.768 --> 00:10:54.130
a target and a transition
plan, and names no framework.

185
00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:58.030
The book already produces all
three: the triaged portfolio,

186
00:10:58.030 --> 00:11:01.055
the reference architecture,
and the wave plan.

187
00:11:01.055 --> 00:11:03.753
So file those artifacts
under the phase and cell

188
00:11:03.753 --> 00:11:06.518
your architects know,
rather than rewriting them.

189
00:11:06.518 --> 00:11:09.031
And don't try to buy
compliance with a framework.

190
00:11:09.031 --> 00:11:10.600
Now, the waves.

191
00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:15.438
A disposition for every
application is a list, not a plan.

192
00:11:15.438 --> 00:11:20.475
Waves turn it into one, inside the
program's existing phases and gates.

193
00:11:20.475 --> 00:11:25.703
Start with a pilot of a few simple
applications, plus one representative hard case,

194
00:11:25.703 --> 00:11:30.163
so the first factory wave doesn't
meet its first tangled database cold.

195
00:11:30.163 --> 00:11:35.150
Group applications by what must move
together, especially a shared database.

196
00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:38.612
Plan a few waves ahead, but
only the next one in detail.

197
00:11:38.612 --> 00:11:42.562
And size the waves by the throughput
you've measured, not by the schedule.

198
00:11:42.562 --> 00:11:47.650
The factory is working if the fifth wave
costs less per application than the first.

199
00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:49.640
Finally, paying for it.

200
00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:52.594
Waves are also a funding structure:

201
00:11:52.594 --> 00:11:56.165
small enough to approve, with
results soon enough to show.

202
00:11:56.165 --> 00:12:01.673
The Technology Modernization Fund wants
proposals that improve, retire or replace

203
00:12:01.673 --> 00:12:06.315
systems, aiming for results within a
year, and a wave plan fits that shape.

204
00:12:06.315 --> 00:12:11.477
Working capital funds let the savings from
one wave's retirements pay for the next.

205
00:12:11.477 --> 00:12:12.640
Two cautions.

206
00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:17.265
Report cost avoided only once the
old system is actually switched off.

207
00:12:17.265 --> 00:12:19.740
And re-score the portfolio every year,

208
00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:22.615
with the budget cycle,
so the list stays true.

209
00:12:22.615 --> 00:12:27.540
The CIO, who advises on every
program, should sign the wave plan.

210
00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:29.760
Let's pull the decisions together.

211
00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:32.085
So, the decisions.

212
00:12:32.085 --> 00:12:35.925
The CIO approves no rewrite that
waits on a single cut-over,

213
00:12:35.925 --> 00:12:39.685
charters the architecture
board, and signs the wave plan.

214
00:12:39.685 --> 00:12:43.305
The program starts with an
inventory and an agreed rubric,

215
00:12:43.305 --> 00:12:46.247
and application owners
sign each disposition.

216
00:12:46.247 --> 00:12:51.061
The chief architect sets one target
stack, asks every refactor how it will be

217
00:12:51.061 --> 00:12:53.047
strangled, forbids dual writes,

218
00:12:53.047 --> 00:12:56.409
and makes no-write agents
with sponsors the standard.

219
00:12:56.409 --> 00:12:59.022
No cut-over happens without three proofs.

220
00:12:59.022 --> 00:13:02.035
Unsupported software is
a finding with a plan.

221
00:13:02.035 --> 00:13:04.742
Privacy and records come
in at design review,

222
00:13:04.742 --> 00:13:09.572
and the authorizing official decides
when a change reopens the authorization.

223
00:13:09.572 --> 00:13:13.684
Book 18 has the detail,
with every claim footnoted.

224
00:13:13.684 --> 00:13:14.500
Thank you.
